VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: SY4
ATTEMPT: 3
---
Scope per manifest: internal/services/metrics/metrics.go (new LivingOutflows, rewritten
Calculate body, +2 test files new/extended), internal/handlers/dashboard/handlers.go
(buildBudgetVsActualChartData rewritten to use metrics.LivingOutflows, +test), no direct edit
to mcpsvc/spend this attempt (transitive fix only). Confirmed via `git diff HEAD` hunk reads on
both files; probes run exclusively via `go test -overlay` per the new run convention — zero
on-disk files added to the repo tree at any point (confirmed via `git status --porcelain`
before writing this verdict).

FIRST: re-ran both prior winning probes through the real, rewritten Calculate (overlay-based).
Both now PASS:
  flagged-net-refund:   LivingExpensesTotal=3000 want 3000; PlanExcludedTotal=-500 want -500
  remainder-net-refund: LivingExpensesTotal=3000 want 3000; PlanExcludedTotal=500  want 500
Ruling SY-2026-08-30c's and SY-2026-08-30d's own counterexamples are both genuinely fixed.

ATTACK 1 — BOTH signs diverging simultaneously (flagged group net refund AND remainder net
refund, same window). Overlay probe: remainder = -1000+4000 (net +3000, refund), flagged =
-2000+2500 (net +500, refund). Result: LivingExpensesTotal=3000 (correct, = |remainder|
exactly), PlanExcludedTotal=-500 (correct sign). The set-exclusion architecture (LivingOutflows
+ ordinary Abs) is robust to arbitrary combined sign permutations at the metrics.Calculate
layer -- this is a genuine, verified improvement over attempts 1-2's subtraction-based shape.
Not a defect.

ATTACK 2 — Per-month granularity with a remainder refund in ONE month only. Overlay probe:
Jan remainder nets +1500 (refund), Feb is ordinary -1500 spend. LivingExpensesTrend =
[1500, 1500] (both months correctly show |month remainder|, Jan unaffected by its own sign
flip). Range total = |sum over ALL living rows in range| = |(-500+2000)+(-1500)| = |0| = 0 --
confirmed this is computed as ONE range-level Abs of the signed sum (matching totalExpenses'
own convention), NOT a naive sum of the per-month Abs trend values (which would wrongly give
3000). Not a defect.

ATTACK 3 — LivingOutflows aliasing/mutation. Overlay probe: captured `outflows.Transactions`
length and signed sum before calling LivingOutflows, called it, re-checked after -- both
identical (len 3->3, sum -3900->-3900). Read the implementation directly: `result :=
&models.TransactionSet{}` is a fresh struct, `append` starts from a nil slice, so no backing
array of the input is ever touched. TotalExpenses from the real Calculate = 3900 (every
dollar), confirmed via the same probe, unaffected by the flag. Not a defect.

ATTACK 4 — summarize_spending transitively. Grepped summary.go directly: `living_monthly_actual`
flows ONLY through `m := metrics.Calculate(filtered, ..., planExclusions)` at line 298; every
other `math.Abs` mention in the file is an unrelated, pre-existing convention for
CategoryTotals/by-month breakdown rows (documented in the file's own doc comments), not a
second implementation of the living figure. No split-classification risk here. Not a defect.

ATTACK 5 — Worker's scope-note claim (CombinedCumulativeBalance's own pre-existing "abs of
parts" precondition break, claimed identical with/without the flag). Re-verified myself via
overlay, NOT by trusting the manifest's transcript. First attempt at reproducing (living-only
fixture, no HI/healthcare component) showed NEITHER breaks -- inconclusive, because a
healthcare-target of 0 trivially prevents any sign divergence between living and healthcare
components. Second attempt, WITH an HI premium row and nonzero healthcare target (the
condition that actually exercises the sign-interaction): both WITH and WITHOUT the flag broke
the invariant by the IDENTICAL magnitude (diff=800 in both cases, to the decimal). This
independently CONFIRMS the worker's claim: this specific invariant break is master-native
(healthcareTotal/livingTotal are each independently Abs'd, unrelated to SY4's flag) and out of
scope per ruling SY-2026-08-30d's own precedent. Correctly omitted from the shipped test suite.
Not a defect -- this is the strongest attack that failed to break the work.

ATTACK 6 — THE CHART'S OWN CUMULATIVE WALK REGRESSED (CONFIRMED DEFECT, NEW THIS ATTEMPT).
Read buildBudgetVsActualChartData's rewritten body: the Living BAR value now correctly calls
`metrics.LivingOutflows` (matches metrics.go, no split classifier). But the walk's `running`
computation was NOT correspondingly rewritten -- it still does
`running += monthTarget - (livingMonth + hcAmt)`, summing TWO INDEPENDENTLY-Abs'd quantities
(livingMonth from the new LivingOutflows-based Abs, hcAmt from the pre-existing healthcare
Abs). On master/attempts 1-2, this was harmless: `livingMonth` was DEFINED as `expAmt - hcAmt`
(a pure algebraic identity), so `livingMonth + hcAmt` collapsed to `expAmt` EXACTLY regardless
of any sign, by cancellation alone -- no Abs-recombination ever actually happened. Attempt 3's
rewrite decoupled `livingMonth` from that identity (it's now an independently-computed
`math.Abs(monthlyLiving[m].SumAmount())`), so the walk's `livingMonth+hcAmt` term is now a
GENUINE sum-of-two-Abs, which diverges from the true `|living+healthcare|` (the quantity
metrics.go's own rewritten walk correctly computes via ONE Abs over its merged
`nonExcludedOutflows` set) whenever living and healthcare have opposite signs within a month --
the EXACT defect class this whole attempt exists to eliminate, reintroduced in the one place
(the chart's walk) that was not rewritten to match.

PROOF (two overlay probes, `go test -overlay`, no on-disk files):
  (a) Direct comparison, chart vs metrics.Calculate, SAME data/target (500 living / 300
      healthcare), one month: living remainder nets +2000 (refund), healthcare nets -300
      (ordinary premium spend), no plan exclusion involved at all (planExclusions=nil -- this
      is not even about the SY4 flag, it is a chart/metrics baseline-consistency regression):
        chart running[0]   = -1500
        metrics CombinedCumulativeBalance[0] = -885.2156057494867
        diff ~= 615 (real dollars, on the dashboard's rendered "Cumulative balance" line)
  (b) Isolated the target-accrual-formula question (chart's `monthTarget` and metrics.go's
      `accrual` use genuinely different formulas for partial/edge months, a separate,
      pre-existing, much smaller effect) by re-running with a NEGLIGIBLE target (0.01/0.01):
        chart running[0]   = -2299.98
        metrics CombinedCumulativeBalance[0] = -1699.9796303901437
        diff ~= 600.00 -- essentially UNCHANGED from (a), confirming the divergence is almost
        entirely `|living|+|healthcare| - |living+healthcare|` = |2000|+|300|-|2000-300| =
        2300-1700 = 600, NOT the target-formula difference (which shrank to ~0.02 as expected
        at this target scale).
  This is provably NEW: on master (and attempts 1-2), `livingMonth+hcAmt` was an exact
  algebraic identity equal to `expAmt` with zero sign-dependence -- the chart's walk and
  metrics.go's walk computed the literal same `spend`/`expAmt` quantity by construction, so no
  divergence of this kind was possible pre-attempt-3, with or without any plan exclusion.

Fix, confined to SY4's own touched file (handlers.go's buildBudgetVsActualChartData): mirror
metrics.go's own fix exactly -- merge the month's living and healthcare transactions into one
set BEFORE taking Abs (analogous to `nonExcludedOutflows`), and use that single Abs value as
the walk's `spend` term, instead of separately Abs'ing `livingMonth` and `hcAmt` and adding them.

Own build/test re-verification: `go test ./internal/services/metrics/...
./internal/handlers/dashboard/... ./internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/... -count=1` all `ok` (the
shipped suite never compares the chart's walk against metrics.Calculate's walk for the same
data, so this divergence has no test coverage in either direction).

STRONGEST ATTACK THAT FAILED TO BREAK IT: Attack 5 (the worker's own scope-note claim about the
pre-existing CombinedCumulativeBalance invariant). This was the attack most likely to produce a
false FAIL if I had trusted the manifest's transcript at face value or used an under-powered
fixture (my own first attempt at it was inconclusive) -- independently re-derived and confirmed
TRUE with a fixture that actually exercises the sign interaction (both break, identical
magnitude 800 to the decimal), correctly leaving it out of scope per ruling SY-2026-08-30d's own
precedent.

VERDICT: FAIL on Attack 6 -- buildBudgetVsActualChartData's cumulative-balance walk still sums
two independently-Abs'd quantities (a "second implementation" of the pattern metrics.go's own
Calculate correctly rewrote this attempt to use ONE Abs over a merged set), producing a
~$600-and-up divergence from the dashboard's own KPI walk whenever living and healthcare (or,
by construction, any bucket split) diverge in sign within a month -- a genuine, newly-introduced
regression, not the pre-existing master-native quirk the manifest correctly identifies
elsewhere.
